## Distributions ## {#dataset-distributions}

[=Consumers=] that are interested in a dataset should be able to access the data in it.
[=Distributions=] tell consumers where and how they can get the data.

> Therefore, publishers *SHOULD* add at least one distribution.
> Each distribution *MUST* have at least a MIME format and the URL where the distribution can be directly accessed.

Examples of distributions are data dumps in one or more RDF serializations, such as JSON-LD and Turtle,
CSV files, SPARQL endpoints, OAI-PMH endpoints or other [=web APIs=].
All distributions of a dataset *MUST* contain broadly the same data.

The URL of the distribution itself – the data file, or the endpoint of the [=web API=] – goes in
`schema:contentUrl` (Schema.org) or `dcat:accessURL` (DCAT-AP-NL 3.0).
For a download, DCAT-AP-NL 3.0 also puts the file URL in `dcat:downloadURL`.
If a documentation page describes or provides access to the distribution alongside it – for example a SPARQL query editor for the endpoint, or a download landing page –
add it on the distribution with `schema:documentation` or `foaf:page`.

The format of the distribution goes in `schema:encodingFormat` or `dcat:mediaType`.
A [=web API=] identifies its protocol instead (see [[#usage-information]]):
in Schema.org with `schema:usageInfo`;
in DCAT-AP-NL 3.0 with `dct:conformsTo`, next to a `dct:format` from the [EU file type list](https://op.europa.eu/en/web/eu-vocabularies/concept-scheme/-/resource?uri=http://publications.europa.eu/resource/authority/file-type).

<div class="example">
    A minimal definition of a SPARQL endpoint distribution.
    In the Schema.org vocabulary, each type of distribution is called a [DataDownload](https://schema.org/DataDownload),
    even if it is a query endpoint.

    <div class="vocab-tabs">
    <pre highlight=json-ld data-vocab="schema">
        {
          "@context": "https://schema.org/",
          "@type": "DataDownload",
          "contentUrl": "http://vocab.getty.edu/sparql",
          "usageInfo": "https://www.w3.org/TR/sparql11-protocol/"
        }
    </pre>
    <pre highlight=json-ld data-vocab="dcat">
        {
          "@context": {
            "dcat": "http://www.w3.org/ns/dcat#",
            "dct": "http://purl.org/dc/terms/"
          },
          "@type": "dcat:Distribution",
          "dcat:accessURL": { "@id": "http://vocab.getty.edu/sparql" },
          "dct:conformsTo": { "@id": "https://www.w3.org/TR/sparql11-protocol/" },
          "dct:format": { "@id": "http://publications.europa.eu/resource/authority/file-type/SPARQLQ" },
          "dct:license": { "@id": "https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/" }
        }
    </pre>
    </div>
</div>

The distributions are then included under the `schema:distribution` or `dcat:distribution` attribute with the dataset.

<div class="example">
    A dataset with one SPARQL and two download distributions.
    The SPARQL endpoint separates the endpoint URL (`schema:contentUrl` or `dcat:accessURL`)
    from its query UI (`schema:documentation` or `foaf:page`).

    <div class="vocab-tabs">
    <pre highlight=json-ld data-vocab="schema">
        {
          "@context": "https://schema.org/",
          "@type": "Dataset",
          "distribution": [
            {
              "@type": "DataDownload",
              "contentUrl": "https://service.archief.nl/sparql",
              "documentation": "https://www.nationaalarchief.nl/onderzoeken/sparql",
              "usageInfo": "https://www.w3.org/TR/sparql11-protocol/"
            },
            {
              "@type": "DataDownload",
              "encodingFormat": "application/ld+json",
              "contentUrl": "http://data.bibliotheken.nl/id/dataset/rise-alba.json"
            },
            {
              "@type": "DataDownload",
              "encodingFormat": "text/csv+gzip",
              "contentUrl": "https://example.com/data.csv.gz"
            }
          ]
        }
    </pre>
    <pre highlight=json-ld data-vocab="dcat">
        {
          "@context": {
            "dcat": "http://www.w3.org/ns/dcat#",
            "dct": "http://purl.org/dc/terms/",
            "foaf": "http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/"
          },
          "@type": "dcat:Dataset",
          "dcat:distribution": [
            {
              "@type": "dcat:Distribution",
              "dcat:accessURL": { "@id": "https://service.archief.nl/sparql" },
              "foaf:page": { "@id": "https://www.nationaalarchief.nl/onderzoeken/sparql" },
              "dct:conformsTo": { "@id": "https://www.w3.org/TR/sparql11-protocol/" },
              "dct:format": { "@id": "http://publications.europa.eu/resource/authority/file-type/SPARQLQ" },
              "dct:license": { "@id": "https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/" }
            },
            {
              "@type": "dcat:Distribution",
              "dcat:mediaType": { "@id": "https://www.iana.org/assignments/media-types/application/ld+json" },
              "dcat:accessURL": { "@id": "http://data.bibliotheken.nl/id/dataset/rise-alba.json" },
              "dcat:downloadURL": { "@id": "http://data.bibliotheken.nl/id/dataset/rise-alba.json" },
              "dct:license": { "@id": "https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/" }
            },
            {
              "@type": "dcat:Distribution",
              "dcat:mediaType": { "@id": "https://www.iana.org/assignments/media-types/text/csv" },
              "dcat:compressFormat": { "@id": "https://www.iana.org/assignments/media-types/application/gzip" },
              "dcat:accessURL": { "@id": "https://example.com/data.csv.gz" },
              "dcat:downloadURL": { "@id": "https://example.com/data.csv.gz" },
              "dct:license": { "@id": "https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/" }
            }
          ]
        }
    </pre>
    </div>
</div>

See [[#distribution-attributes]] for a full overview.

<div class="advisement">
    In [[#v2|v2.0]] of this specification, <code>schema:contentUrl</code> *MUST* be an IRI.
    String and <code>xsd:anyURI</code> literals will no longer be accepted.
    Versions up to 1.15.1 of this specification said the reverse here – that <code>contentUrl</code> must become an <code>xsd:anyURI</code> literal – which was a mistake.
    A <code>contentUrl</code> written as a JSON string in JSON-LD with the Schema.org context, as in the example above, is read as an IRI and stays valid.
    In DCAT-AP-NL 3.0, write <code>dcat:accessURL</code> and <code>dcat:downloadURL</code> as IRIs, with <code>@id</code> in JSON-LD.
</div>

### Creation and modification dates ### {#distribution-date}

Publishers *SHOULD* make known when the [=distribution=] was first published and when it was last updated,
so [=consumers=] can efficiently stay up-to-date with the latest changes.
Please note that this is different from the [[#dataset-date|dataset description’s dates]].
Datetimes (`2019-08-15T08:05:00Z`) are preferred because they offer greater precision,
but simple dates (`2019-04-14`) are also allowed.

<div class="example">
    Specify distribution dates:

    <div class="vocab-tabs">
    <pre highlight=json-ld data-vocab="schema">
        {
          "@context": "https://schema.org/",
          "@type": "DataDownload",
          "datePublished": "2019-04-14",
          "dateModified": "2019-08-15T08:05:00"
        }
    </pre>
    <pre highlight=json-ld data-vocab="dcat">
        {
          "@context": {
            "dcat": "http://www.w3.org/ns/dcat#",
            "dct": "http://purl.org/dc/terms/",
            "xsd": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#"
          },
          "@type": "dcat:Distribution",
          "dct:issued": { "@value": "2019-04-14", "@type": "xsd:date" },
          "dct:modified": { "@value": "2019-08-15T08:05:00", "@type": "xsd:dateTime" }
        }
    </pre>
    </div>
</div>

### Usage information ### {#usage-information}

Each distribution *SHOULD* include one or more [schema:usageInfo](https://schema.org/usageInfo) (Schema.org)
or [dct:conformsTo](http://purl.org/dc/terms/conformsTo) (DCAT-AP-NL 3.0) IRIs
that describe what the distribution provides.

For [=web APIs=], this *MUST* be the IRI of the protocol specification (such as SPARQL or OAI-PMH).
The Dataset Register uses this IRI to identify the distribution as an API.

For downloads as well as APIs, this *MUST* be the IRI of the application profile(s) that the data conforms to.

