Tools {#tools}
=============

*This section is non-normative.*

Developers can use the [NDE Register API](https://datasetregister.netwerkdigitaalerfgoed.nl/api/) to validate datasets and catalogs against the requirements described in this document.
The [[SHACL]] shape graph used to validate datasets and catalogs is available at [/shacl](https://datasetregister.netwerkdigitaalerfgoed.nl/api/shacl).

## Running the shapes yourself ## {#validating-yourself}

The Schema.org shapes target properties in the `https://schema.org/` namespace.
Schema.org’s own JSON-LD context maps its terms to `http://schema.org/`,
so a Schema.org [=dataset description=] validated against that context matches no shape at all
and the report comes back `sh:conforms true` without anything having been checked.
Since Schema.org release 30.1 that context also no longer types `mainEntityOfPage`, `license`
and the date properties, which turns values that should be IRIs and dates into plain strings.

To validate your own file – and only to validate it, not to publish it –
point its `@context` at [https://def.nde.nl/context.jsonld](https://def.nde.nl/context.jsonld).
That context declares the same prefixes as Schema.org’s
and restores the typings the shapes rely on,
so you get the report the [Register API](https://datasetregister.netwerkdigitaalerfgoed.nl/api/) gives you.

<div class="example">
    <xmp highlight=json>
        {
          "@context": "https://def.nde.nl/context.jsonld",
          "@id": "https://example.org/dataset",
          "@type": "Dataset",
          "name": {"@value": "My dataset", "@language": "nl"},
          "mainEntityOfPage": "https://example.org/about-this-dataset"
        }
    </xmp>
</div>

Keep serving your [=dataset description=] itself with `"@context": "https://schema.org/"`:
the Register substitutes the context on its side when it reads your description,
so there is nothing to change in what you publish.

DCAT-AP-NL 3.0 descriptions need no such step.
Their JSON-LD declares the namespaces itself, as in the examples in this document,
so you can validate them as they are.

Google's [Rich Results Test](https://search.google.com/test/rich-results) (previously Structured Data Testing Tool)
can help with testing RDF metadata in general.

